{"name":"Red Carpet","hex":"#bc2026","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"red carpet","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A strip of red carpet that is laid for a VIP to walk on.","VIP treatment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red_carpet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Red carpet","blocks":[{"text":"A red carpet is traditionally used to mark the route taken by heads of state on ceremonial and formal occasions, and has in recent decades been extended to use by VIPs and celebrities at formal events."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Pi%C3%B1era_Paris_arrival_2010.jpg/330px-Pi%C3%B1era_Paris_arrival_2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_carpet","wikidata":"Q288862"},{"title":"Red Carpet (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Red Carpet\" is a song recorded by Japanese singer Namie Amuro. It was released on December 2, 2015 via Avex Trax and her label Dimension Point as the singer's third consecutive non-album single, being supported by B-side track \"Black Make-Up\". \"Red Carpet\" was distributed in two physical formats—a standard CD, and a CD and DVD bundle—and was made available for digital consumption. The recording was written and composed by Matthew Tishler, Paula Winger, Stephanie Lewis and Tiger, whilst production was handled by Tishler. Musically, the track features guitars, synthesizers, keyboards and drums as instrumentation. Lyrically, the songwriting focuses on themes of self-confidence, empowerment and narcissism, and uses the title as a metaphor for life experiences."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/70/Namie_Amuro_-_Red_Carpet_single_cover.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Carpet_(song)","wikidata":"Q21714441"},{"title":"Carpet","blocks":[{"text":"A carpet or rug is a textile floor covering that typically consists of an upper layer of pile attached to a backing. In Europe, the pile was traditionally made of wool, but since the 20th century, synthetic fibres such as polypropylene, nylon, and polyester have been used as less expensive alternatives. The pile often consists of twisted tufts that are heat-treated to maintain their structure."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Wollteppich_1.jpg/330px-Wollteppich_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carpet","wikidata":"Q163446"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Barack_Obama_leaving_Air_Force_One_by_night.jpg/500px-Barack_Obama_leaving_Air_Force_One_by_night.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Barack_Obama_leaving_Air_Force_One_by_night.jpg/1280px-Barack_Obama_leaving_Air_Force_One_by_night.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Barack_Obama_leaving_Air_Force_One_by_night.jpg